WebGPU: GPU timer results in the browser with a device from PlatformData::context

In the browser, bgfx creates an instance of its own for a device the application passes in
PlatformData::context, and emdawnwebgpu files that device's callbacks under the instance it was imported
into: none, for emscripten_webgpu_get_device. bgfx waits for the timer readback with an AllowProcessEvents
callback and processes only its own instance, so the callback never ran, the readback stayed pending, and no
timer query was resolved again: every GPU time stayed 0. For such a device the callback is now spontaneous;
bgfx is single-threaded in the browser, so it runs from the event loop, between frames. A device bgfx
creates itself is on bgfx's instance, which submit() processes every frame, and keeps AllowProcessEvents.

diff --git a/src/renderer_webgpu.cpp b/src/renderer_webgpu.cpp
--- a/src/renderer_webgpu.cpp
+++ b/src/renderer_webgpu.cpp
@@ -6996,6 +6996,12 @@ WGPU_IMPORT
 		m_resolved   = false;
 		m_mapPending = true;
 
+		// In the browser bgfx creates an instance of its own for a device the application created
+		// (PlatformData::context), and that device's callbacks are not on it: emdawnwebgpu files them under
+		// the instance the device was imported into (none, for emscripten_webgpu_get_device). bgfx is
+		// single-threaded there, so a spontaneous callback runs from the browser's event loop, between frames.
+		const bool externalBrowserDevice = BX_ENABLED(BX_PLATFORM_EMSCRIPTEN) && NULL != g_platformData.context;
+
 		WGPU_CHECK(wgpuBufferMapAsync(
 			  m_readback
 			, WGPUMapMode_Read
@@ -7003,7 +7009,10 @@ WGPU_IMPORT
 			, size_t(getBufferSize() )
 			, {
 				.nextInChain = NULL,
-				.mode        = WGPUCallbackMode_AllowProcessEvents,
+				.mode        = externalBrowserDevice
+					? WGPUCallbackMode_AllowSpontaneous
+					: WGPUCallbackMode_AllowProcessEvents
+					,
 				.callback    = readTimestampResultsCb,
 				.userdata1   = this,
 				.userdata2   = NULL,
